---
// Newsletter page - renders pure HTML content
const htmlContent = `<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
<meta http-equiv="X-UA-Compatible" content="IE=edge">
<title>Resolved &middot; Issue 9 &middot; Postponed, not paused</title>
<style type="text/css">
  .preheader { display:none !important; visibility:hidden; opacity:0; color:transparent; height:0; width:0; overflow:hidden; mso-hide:all; font-size:1px; line-height:1px; max-height:0; max-width:0; }
</style>
</head>
<body style="margin:0; padding:0; background-color:#F5F5F5;">

<!-- PREHEADER -->
<div class="preheader">Swift moved the date. It didn&rsquo;t move the requirement, and four corridors still go live on 14 November.</div>

<table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="background-color:#F5F5F5; margin:0; padding:0;">
  <tr>
    <td align="center" style="padding:0;">

      <table role="presentation" width="600" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="width:100%; max-width:600px; background-color:#FFFFFF; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">

        <!-- ============ BANNER ============ -->
        <tr>
          <td align="center" style="background-color:#0D1E42; padding:0; font-size:0; line-height:0;">
            <img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dsr6vlfn1/image/upload/v1791386216/newsletter-9-banner_wqg3jf.png"
                 width="600"
                 height="250"
                 alt="Resolved &middot; Postponed, not paused &middot; by ioNova AI"
                 border="0"
                 style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:600px;height:auto;">
          </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- ============ SECTION 1 &mdash; THE LEAD ============ -->
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:32px 36px; border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">

            <h1 style="margin:0 0 20px 0; font-size:24px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E; letter-spacing:-0.3px; line-height:1.3; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">Postponed, not paused: how to use the time Swift bought on the ISO 20022 deadline</h1>
            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">In September, <strong>51% of debtor addresses and 53% of creditor addresses</strong> on the Swift network were not properly structured, according to Swift network data cited at Sibos 2026. Had the November rule gone ahead, at least 60% of payments would have been rejected.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">So Swift postponed the ISO 20022 deadline for structured addresses. Then, in three sessions over three days at Sibos, three of its senior executives told the industry the same thing: don&rsquo;t wait.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">They&rsquo;re right. A deadline pushes every programme toward the minimum: populate the field, pass the validator, close the ticket. The postponement lifts that pressure. <strong><em>Do it right. Do it once. Do it fully.</em></strong> The banks that use the time that way will gain the most, above all in compliance.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 24px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Here&rsquo;s where things stand, why the date moved, what Swift asked of banks, and how to spend the time.</p>
            <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="background-color:#232F3E; border-radius:4px;">
              <tr>
                <td style="padding:20px 24px;">
                  <p style="margin:0 0 10px 0; font-size:17px; line-height:1.6; color:#FFFFFF; font-weight:700; font-style:italic;">Institutions &ldquo;should strive to adopt these address formats now.&rdquo;</p>
                  <p style="margin:0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; color:#5FCFD0; font-style:italic;">&mdash; Swift, Knowledge Centre article 6001119, 21 September 2026</p>
                </td>
              </tr>
            </table>

          </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- ============ SECTION 2 &mdash; WHERE THE DEADLINE STANDS ============ -->
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:32px 36px; border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">

            <h2 style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:22px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E; letter-spacing:-0.3px; line-height:1.3; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">Where the ISO 20022 structured address deadline stands</h2>

            <p style="margin:0 0 20px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">There is no new Swift date yet. <a href="https://www2.swift.com/knowledgecentre/kb_articles/6001119" style="color:#0972D3; text-decoration:none; font-weight:700;">Swift says</a> it will define a new timeline by December 2026. Until then there is <em>&ldquo;no mandatory requirement&rdquo;</em> to use structured or hybrid addresses, yet institutions <em>&ldquo;should strive to adopt these address formats now.&rdquo;</em> <strong>The ISO 20022 address requirements haven&rsquo;t changed. Their date has.</strong></p>
            <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 22px 0;">
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>14 November 2026:</strong> SR 2026 will not proceed on Swift. The requirement is <em>&ldquo;rescheduled, not abandoned.&rdquo;</em> Four corridors keep the date.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>By December 2026:</strong> Swift&rsquo;s new timeline.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>12 June 2027:</strong> the deferred SR 2026 release. No CBPR+ changes. <strong>Not your deadline.</strong></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>November 2027:</strong> Swift&rsquo;s next payments release. The address change isn&rsquo;t confirmed for it.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>2029&ndash;2030:</strong> FATF Recommendation 16, implemented by market infrastructures in 2029 and in national law by 2030.</td>
              </tr>
            </table>
            <p style="margin:0 0 24px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Structured and hybrid addresses are <em>&ldquo;already supported as part of SR 2025&rdquo;</em>. Nothing stops you starting.</p>
            <img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dsr6vlfn1/image/upload/v1791386238/image_02_1600x800_fodz67.png"
                 width="528"
                 alt="Timeline of five dates. 14 Nov 2026: still a deadline in Korea, the Philippines and Taiwan, and in China in November. By Dec 2026: Swift defines its new timeline. 12 Jun 2027: not your deadline, no CBPR+ changes. Nov 2027: Swift&#x27;s next payments release, address change unconfirmed. 2029 to 2030: FATF Recommendation 16."
                 border="0"
                 style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:528px;height:auto;border-radius:4px;margin:0 0 10px 0;">
            <p style="margin:0 0 0px 0; font-size:14px; line-height:1.6; color:#466587; font-style:italic; text-align:center;">The dates to plan against. Sources: Swift KB 6001119 and notice of 21 September 2026; PMPG/HVPS+ overview, status as at 6 September 2026; FATF; HVPS+ at Sibos 2026. Status as checked 7 October 2026.</p>

          </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- ============ SECTION 3 &mdash; FOUR CORRIDORS ============ -->
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:32px 36px; border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">

            <h2 style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:22px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E; letter-spacing:-0.3px; line-height:1.3; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">14 November is still a deadline in four corridors</h2>

            <p style="margin:0 0 20px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">On Swift, <strong><em>the clock moved. The requirement didn&rsquo;t.</em></strong> In four corridors, the clock didn&rsquo;t move either. According to PMPG&rsquo;s overview with HVPS+ and Swift (status as at 6 September), BOK-Wire+ in Korea, PhilPaSSplus in the Philippines and FISC in Taiwan remove fully unstructured addresses on <strong>14 November 2026</strong>, and CIPS in China does so in November.</p>
            <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 24px 0;">
              <tr>
                <td style="border-left:3px solid #43ADC1; background-color:#F5F9FF; border-top:1px solid #D0DAEB; border-right:1px solid #D0DAEB; border-bottom:1px solid #D0DAEB; border-radius:4px; padding:16px 20px;">
                  <p style="margin:0 0 8px 0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.7; color:#466587; font-style:italic;">&ldquo;The destination PMI may reject a payment where the fully unstructured postal-address option is no longer accepted.&rdquo;</p>
                  <p style="margin:0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; color:#466587; font-style:italic;">&mdash; PMPG market practice, September 2026</p>
                </td>
              </tr>
            </table>
            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Each rejection becomes a repair, a return or an investigation, at the <strong>$25&ndash;50 per exception</strong> used in our published cost work.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Europe made two decisions, not one. The European Payments Council delayed the end of unstructured addresses in SEPA, with a new date due from its October board. The Eurosystem and EBA CLEARING deploy T2 and EURO1 on 28 November and keep accepting them.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 20px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">PMPG&rsquo;s overview, published with HVPS+ and Swift, lists 26 infrastructures and the position each has published. No list covers your correspondents, so ask each one:</p>
            <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 0px 0;">
              <tr>
                <td width="24" valign="top" style="width:24px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>1.</strong></td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">What will you accept?</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="24" valign="top" style="width:24px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>2.</strong></td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">What will you reject?</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="24" valign="top" style="width:24px; padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>3.</strong></td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">What will you do with an address you can&rsquo;t parse: repair it, return it, or pass it on?</td>
              </tr>
            </table>

          </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- ============ SECTION 4 &mdash; WHY SWIFT POSTPONED ============ -->
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:32px 36px; border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">

            <h2 style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:22px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E; letter-spacing:-0.3px; line-height:1.3; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">Why Swift postponed: the messages were ready, the data wasn&rsquo;t</h2>

            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Swift&rsquo;s chief operations officer called the decision risk-led. Going ahead would have been lose-lose, said the head of ISO 20022 at Federal Reserve Financial Services: mass rejections, or poor data forced into structured fields just to pass validation.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 24px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Swift&rsquo;s head of the ISO 20022 programme asked whether the industry had focused on message readiness rather than data readiness. In the Fed&rsquo;s three phases, the industry finished phase one, the standard, in 2025. It wasn&rsquo;t ready for phase two.</p>
            <img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dsr6vlfn1/image/upload/v1791386239/image_03_1600x800_yzn8n2.png"
                 width="528"
                 alt="Three phases of ISO 20022. Phase 1, implement the standard: done in 2025. Phase 2, structure the data: where the industry is now. Phase 3, improve data quality: ahead. Banner: Migration doesn&#x27;t clean data. Someone has to."
                 border="0"
                 style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:528px;height:auto;border-radius:4px;margin:0 0 10px 0;">
            <p style="margin:0 0 24px 0; font-size:14px; line-height:1.6; color:#466587; font-style:italic; text-align:center;">Three phases, as described by Federal Reserve Financial Services at the Sibos 2026 Standards Forum.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 20px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">The reasons the panels gave had nothing to do with schemas:</p>
            <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 26px 0;">
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">The data lives outside payments, in onboarding, KYC, core banking and corporate ERP. One bank ready for November still had to change <strong>250 back-office systems</strong>.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Hybrid gave false comfort. It still needs town and country, and on the creditor side that data belongs to the client&rsquo;s clients.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Corporates were reached last, and were told the change was for sanctions screening.</td>
              </tr>
            </table>
            <p style="margin:0 0 26px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">In thirty years of payments compliance, I&rsquo;ve seen it in every migration: the format moves on the date; the data moves when someone owns it. <strong>Migration doesn&rsquo;t clean data. Someone has to.</strong></p>
            <h3 style="margin:8px 0 14px 0; font-size:17px; font-weight:700; color:#232F3E; line-height:1.4; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">Our record, including the part that didn&rsquo;t hold</h3>
            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">In March we called this <em>&ldquo;a data-quality problem wearing a messaging costume.&rdquo;</em> On 6 August, in Issue #8 of this newsletter, I published the headline <em>&ldquo;62% of Payment Messages Will Fail the ISO 20022 Deadline on 15 November 2026.&rdquo;</em> The 62% was our own estimate. Swift&rsquo;s September data put the share of payments that would have been rejected at 60% or more: a different method, the same order of magnitude. The date in that headline did not hold.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 0px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>We called the failure mode. We did not call the timing.</strong></p>

          </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- ============ SECTION 5 &mdash; WHAT SWIFT TOLD BANKS ============ -->
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:32px 36px; border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">

            <h2 style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:22px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E; letter-spacing:-0.3px; line-height:1.3; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">What Swift told banks at Sibos: don&rsquo;t wait</h2>

            <p style="margin:0 0 20px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">The most consistent message at Sibos came from Swift itself, and it matched the written line above. On stage, three of its executives said it in their own words:</p>
            <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 26px 0;">
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>Swift&rsquo;s chief operations officer:</strong> structured addresses work today on all networks. If you&rsquo;re ready, go ahead without waiting for the rest of the community.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>A senior Swift standards executive:</strong> if you are ready, switch it on; every institution that does helps the rest of the industry.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>Swift&rsquo;s chief executive for the UK and Ireland:</strong> same destination, new timeline.</td>
              </tr>
            </table>
            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Next time, Swift wants adoption to show on the network before a change becomes mandatory, and it will watch adoption around 14 November, hoping to see it climb sharply. If the next date depends on what Swift sees, the banks that switch on now are writing the evidence it will be set on.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 0px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Speakers proposed three-to-five-year roadmaps. But four corridors go live in November, FATF Recommendation 16 lands in 2029, and the Fed&rsquo;s test is that unstructured addresses are gone by 2030. <em>You have more time than you had in August. You don&rsquo;t have as much as it looks.</em></p>

          </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- ============ SECTION 6 &mdash; DO IT RIGHT, ONCE, FULLY ============ -->
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:32px 36px; border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">

            <h2 style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:22px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E; letter-spacing:-0.3px; line-height:1.3; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">Use the time to do it right, do it once and do it fully</h2>

            <p style="margin:0 0 20px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">The deadline was the strongest force pushing programmes toward the minimum. That force has eased. PMPG put the alternative in one sentence:</p>
            <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 24px 0;">
              <tr>
                <td style="border-left:3px solid #43ADC1; background-color:#F5F9FF; border-top:1px solid #D0DAEB; border-right:1px solid #D0DAEB; border-bottom:1px solid #D0DAEB; border-radius:4px; padding:16px 20px;">
                  <p style="margin:0 0 8px 0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.7; color:#466587; font-style:italic; font-weight:700;">&ldquo;Additional time only creates value if it is used to improve readiness.&rdquo;</p>
                  <p style="margin:0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; color:#466587; font-style:italic;">&mdash; PMPG</p>
                </td>
              </tr>
            </table>
            <img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dsr6vlfn1/image/upload/v1791386238/image_04_1600x800_lek1rb.png"
                 width="528"
                 alt="Three tests for the time the postponement buys. Do it right: fix it at the source. Do it once: one resolution, every use. Do it fully: every element, every party. Maximum benefit: compliance first."
                 border="0"
                 style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:528px;height:auto;border-radius:4px;margin:0 0 10px 0;">
            <p style="margin:0 0 26px 0; font-size:14px; line-height:1.6; color:#466587; font-style:italic; text-align:center;">Three tests for the time the postponement buys.</p>
            <h3 style="margin:0px 0 14px 0; font-size:17px; font-weight:700; color:#232F3E; line-height:1.4; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">Do it right: fix it at the source</h3>
            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">The address isn&rsquo;t created in the payment message. It&rsquo;s captured at onboarding, held in KYC and core banking and, for most beneficiaries, typed into a corporate&rsquo;s ERP. The further from the source you fix it, the more it costs.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">So fix it where it lives, to a written standard. PMPG&rsquo;s market practice says a repair should be <em>&ldquo;deterministic, transparent&rdquo;</em>, with the original <em>&ldquo;retained for audit and investigation purposes&rdquo;</em>, and no town or country created or assumed where it can&rsquo;t be reliably identified.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong><em>In flight today, at rest once.</em></strong> Fixing at the source doesn&rsquo;t mean waiting for the source. Resolve every payment in flight now, so your November payments aren&rsquo;t the ones rejected, and remediate the records at rest once and fully, so the in-flight repair is needed less every month. Different teams: run them in parallel.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 26px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">AI can help, but the Sibos AI panel agreed that 99.9% accuracy isn&rsquo;t good enough in payments. Its test for any tool: the same input gives the same output, it reports its confidence, it leaves a field blank rather than guess, and a person reviews the result.</p>
            <h3 style="margin:8px 0 14px 0; font-size:17px; font-weight:700; color:#232F3E; line-height:1.4; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">Do it once: one resolution, every use</h3>
            <p style="margin:0 0 24px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Payments, screening, KYC and master data each ask something different of the same address. Fix it separately at each point of use and you fix it eight times, eight slightly different ways. Resolve it once, keep the original, and let every system use the same answer.</p>
            <img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dsr6vlfn1/image/upload/v1791386239/image_05_1000x500_laqja4.png"
                 width="528"
                 alt="Resolve each address once. Use it everywhere. One resolved address, with the original kept for audit, serving cross-border payments, domestic and instant schemes, sanctions screening, AML monitoring, KYC and onboarding, customer master data, reporting and FATF R16, and AI and analytics."
                 border="0"
                 style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:528px;height:auto;border-radius:4px;margin:0 0 10px 0;">
            <div style="height:14px; line-height:14px; font-size:0;">&nbsp;</div>
            <p style="margin:0 0 26px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">It also means not doing it twice: go straight to fully structured, as the Central Bank of The Bahamas told its market, and do the R16 party data in the same pass. <em>You&rsquo;ll open the same customer records either way. Open them once.</em></p>
            <h3 style="margin:8px 0 14px 0; font-size:17px; font-weight:700; color:#232F3E; line-height:1.4; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">Do it fully: every element, every party</h3>
            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">In the CPMI&rsquo;s February 2026 review of 48 payment systems, fully structured was the only address format every ISO 20022 system accepted. Seven systems built on Swift&rsquo;s ISO 20022 Accelerator Pack didn&rsquo;t accept hybrid at all.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 0px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Hybrid remains valid, with no end date. But PMPG says <em>&ldquo;fully structured remains the preferred option&rdquo;</em>, and the EPC recommends moving <em>&ldquo;directly from unstructured to fully structured addresses wherever possible.&rdquo;</em> And &ldquo;fully&rdquo; means every party, both ways: debtors and creditors, sent and received.</p>

          </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- ============ SECTION 7 &mdash; COMPLIANCE GETS THE BIGGEST RETURN ============ -->
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:32px 36px; border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">

            <h2 style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:22px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E; letter-spacing:-0.3px; line-height:1.3; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">Compliance gets the biggest return</h2>

            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">The case for compliance came from Swift, not a vendor. Its head of the ISO 20022 programme called compliance one of the greatest drivers for structured data.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">A managing director in Deloitte&rsquo;s financial-crime practice explained why. Screening unstructured addresses misses critical information and floods analysts with false positives. Studies she cited, without naming them, expect structured addresses to cut false positives by <strong>25&ndash;30%</strong>.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 24px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">That&rsquo;s the first rung of the ladder we published in April: 25&ndash;30% from address fields alone, 40&ndash;60% with structured party data, up to 70% at best. <a href="https://ionova.ai/blog/compliance-dividend-iso-20022-structured-addresses?utm_source=newsletter" style="color:#0972D3; text-decoration:none; font-weight:700;">The Compliance Dividend</a> sets out the case; the Sibos figure reached the same rung independently.</p>
            <img src="https://res.cloudinary.com/dsr6vlfn1/image/upload/v1791386239/image_06_1600x900_fvd4ec.png"
                 width="528"
                 alt="One address in three formats. Unstructured: no country element at all. Hybrid: country and town match exactly. Fully structured: every element can be matched, down to street and building."
                 border="0"
                 style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:528px;height:auto;border-radius:4px;margin:0 0 10px 0;">
            <p style="margin:0 0 24px 0; font-size:14px; line-height:1.6; color:#466587; font-style:italic; text-align:center;">The more structure, the more screening can see. Illustration; the country element carries the ISO 3166 two-letter code.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Hybrid gives screening an exact country and town. Fully structured adds street, building, district and postcode: the elements that tell one of the 28 places called Paris from another.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 20px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">Three things make ISO 20022 compliance work the natural place to start:</p>
            <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 26px 0;">
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>The benefit lands inside the bank.</strong> That&rsquo;s why &ldquo;it&rsquo;s for sanctions screening&rdquo; failed as the pitch to corporates.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>The data is in reach.</strong> Your own customers&rsquo; addresses are already in KYC.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>R16 makes it urgent.</strong> The LEI it asks for adds to name and address; it doesn&rsquo;t replace them.</td>
              </tr>
            </table>
            <p style="margin:0 0 0px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>51% and 53% aren&rsquo;t a reason to wait. They&rsquo;re the size of the job.</strong></p>

          </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- ============ SECTION 8 &mdash; YOUR FIRST STEPS THIS QUARTER ============ -->
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:32px 36px; border-bottom:1px solid #E8E8E8;">

            <h2 style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:22px; font-weight:900; color:#232F3E; letter-spacing:-0.3px; line-height:1.3; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">Your first steps this quarter</h2>

            <p style="margin:0 0 20px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>First, the one thing with a November consequence.</strong> Swift recommends uninstalling the SR 2026 FINplus and Funds Universal Standards Archive packages from production, because the archive <em>&ldquo;becomes active automatically&rdquo;</em> on its activation date. Swift&rsquo;s guidance and the affected packages are in <a href="https://www2.swift.com/knowledgecentre/kb_articles/6001119" style="color:#0972D3; text-decoration:none; font-weight:700;">Knowledge Centre article 6001119</a> (swift.com login may be required).</p>
            <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 22px 0;">
              <tr>
                <td width="24" valign="top" style="width:24px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>1.</strong></td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>Fund one data programme</strong>, with one accountable owner from onboarding to compliance.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="24" valign="top" style="width:24px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>2.</strong></td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>Before 14 November</strong>, switch on for the four corridors and put the three questions to your correspondents.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="24" valign="top" style="width:24px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>3.</strong></td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>Make fully structured the default</strong>, and ask every infrastructure and correspondent: <em>do you accept hybrid?</em></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="24" valign="top" style="width:24px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>4.</strong></td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>Fix at the source while resolving in flight</strong>, debtor side first, then work the beneficiary data with your corporates.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="24" valign="top" style="width:24px; padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>5.</strong></td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>Let compliance lead.</strong> Baseline today&rsquo;s false-positive rate, and plan the address and R16 party data as one pass for 2029.</td>
              </tr>
            </table>
            <p style="margin:0 0 24px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><em>A disclosure: ioNova builds address resolution software. Judge it, and any other tool or model, against the Sibos test above, PMPG&rsquo;s repair principles and <a href="https://ionova.ai/newsletter/how-to-evaluate-an-iso-20022-address-solution?utm_source=newsletter" style="color:#0972D3; text-decoration:none; font-weight:700;">the 25 criteria we have published</a>, not against our word.</em></p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 20px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&#128197; <strong>Know your own 51% and 53% before Swift measures them.</strong> The exposure diagnostic maps your corridors and your correspondents, with every position sourced and dated. Our free readiness baseline is open to the first twelve institutions that request it. &rarr; <a href="https://ionova.ai?utm_source=newsletter" style="color:#0972D3; text-decoration:none; font-weight:700;">ioNova.ai</a>, or reply to this email.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 26px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>Read the full article &rarr;</strong> <a href="https://ionova.ai/blog/iso-20022-structured-address-deadline?utm_source=newsletter" style="color:#0972D3; text-decoration:none; font-weight:700;">ISO 20022 structured addresses: postponed, not paused</a>, with all eight figures and the FAQ.</p>
            <h3 style="margin:8px 0 14px 0; font-size:17px; font-weight:700; color:#232F3E; line-height:1.4; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">What I&rsquo;m watching</h3>
            <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" style="margin:0 0 20px 0;">
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>14 November:</strong> four corridors go live, and Swift measures who has switched on voluntarily.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0 0 12px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>Swift&rsquo;s new timeline:</strong> a firm date, as Deutsche Bank asked at Sibos, or a roadmap with readiness criteria?</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td width="18" valign="top" style="width:18px; padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">&bull;</td>
                <td valign="top" style="padding:0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>The EPC&rsquo;s October decision</strong> on a new SEPA end date.</td>
              </tr>
            </table>
            <p style="margin:0 0 26px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">The test is the Fed&rsquo;s: if unstructured addresses are still around in 2030, the industry has missed the mark. Each of these will be reported here as it lands, sourced and dated.</p>
            <h3 style="margin:8px 0 14px 0; font-size:17px; font-weight:700; color:#232F3E; line-height:1.4; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">Quick answers</h3>
            <p style="margin:0 0 16px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>Is the structured address mandatory in ISO 20022?</strong> Not yet on Swift. There is no mandatory requirement until Swift confirms a new date, but it urges institutions to <em>&ldquo;strive to adopt&rdquo;</em> structured or hybrid addresses now.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 16px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>What is the new Swift structured address deadline?</strong> There isn&rsquo;t one yet. Swift says it will define a new timeline by December 2026. November 2027 is its next payments release, with the address change unconfirmed; 12 June 2027 has no CBPR+ changes.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 16px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>Is 14 November 2026 still a deadline anywhere?</strong> Yes: in Korea (BOK-Wire+), the Philippines (PhilPaSSplus) and Taiwan (FISC), and in China (CIPS) in November. On the Swift network, nothing changes that day.</p>
            <p style="margin:0 0 28px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><strong>What is a Swift hybrid address?</strong> Structured town name and country, plus up to two free-text address lines. PMPG calls it a valid long-term option, but seven systems in the CPMI&rsquo;s 2026 review don&rsquo;t accept it.</p>
            <h3 style="margin:0px 0 14px 0; font-size:17px; font-weight:700; color:#232F3E; line-height:1.4; font-family:Calibri, Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif;">Next issue</h3>
            <p style="margin:0 0 0px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;"><em>Both ends of the bank:</em> the corporates who create most of your inbound address data, and the correspondents who decide what happens to an address they can&rsquo;t parse.</p>

          </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- ============ SIGN-OFF ============ -->
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:32px 36px;">

            <p style="margin:0 0 18px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.7; color:#232F3E;">To your AI success,</p>

            <p style="margin:0 0 4px 0; font-size:16px; line-height:1.6; color:#232F3E; font-weight:700;">Parth Desai</p>

            <p style="margin:0 0 24px 0; font-size:14px; line-height:1.6; color:#466587;">Founder &middot; 30+ Years Enterprise AI in Payments &amp; Financial Services &middot; Leads ioNova&rsquo;s ISO 20022 address-resolution work</p>

            <p style="margin:0 0 14px 0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.7; color:#466587; font-style:italic;">P.S. Know someone whose programme plan says &ldquo;awaiting new date&rdquo;? Forward them this issue. If they settle into China, Korea, the Philippines or Taiwan, their date is still November.</p>

            <p style="margin:0; font-size:15px; line-height:1.7; color:#466587; font-style:italic;">P.P.S. Swift says it will set its new timeline by December. We&rsquo;ll report it here, sourced and dated, and we won&rsquo;t guess at it before then.</p>

          </td>
        </tr>

        <!-- ============ FOOTER ============ -->
        <tr>
          <td align="center" style="background-color:#F8F8F8; padding:24px 36px;">

